tographic hash function \emph{H} as an ideal primitive. In the ROM, the
only way to ``compute'' the hash \emph{H}(\emph{x}) of any value
\emph{x} is by making a \emph{query} to an imaginary entity, the
\emph{random oracle} (RO), which has chosen \emph{H} uniformly at random
from the set of \emph{all} functions with the considered domain and
range.

The hope is that if a cryptographic scheme is secure in the ROM then it
is also secure in the standard model, as long as \emph{H} is
instantiated with a ``good enough'' cryptographic hash function. Even
though in general we cannot hope to obtain provable security in the
standard model in this way, (since there exist artificial counter
examples {[}CGH04{]}), this approach works extremely well in practice,
leading to very efficient schemes that tend to resist all known attacks.

What makes the ROM particularly convenient is that in the security proof
of a cryptographic scheme, we can control the RO. For instance, simply
by recording the queries that the adversary makes to the RO, we know
exactly which hash values he knows, and the hash value
\emph{H}(\emph{x}) is random to him for any \emph{x} that he has not
queried. Furthermore, we can \emph{reprogram} the RO, meaning that we
can let \emph{H}(\emph{x}) be some particular value \emph{y} for some
specific \emph{x}, as long as it is random from the adversary's
perspective.

When considering a \emph{quantum} adversary, the picture changes a bit.
In order to model that such an adversary can evaluate any hash function
\emph{in superposition} on different inputs, we must allow such a
quantum
