\textbf{Remark 5} We allow the set of \emph{instances X}, the set of
\emph{witnesses W} and the relation \emph{R} to depend on a security
parameter \emph{η}. Similarly, the interactive algorithms \emph{P} and
\emph{V} may depend on \emph{η} (or have \emph{η} as part of their
input). However, for ease of notation, we suppress these dependencies on
\emph{η} unless they are crucial.

\textbf{Remark 6} We do not necessarily require a Σ-protocol to be
perfectly or statistically correct. This allows us to include protocols
that use \emph{rejection sampling}, where with a constant probability,
the value \emph{z} would leak too much information on the witness
\emph{w} and so the prover sends \emph{⊥}instead. On the other hand, by
default we consider the soundness/knowledge error to be negligible,
i.e., a dishonest prover succeeds only with negligible probability to
make the verifier accept if \emph{x} is not a valid instance or the
prover has no witness for it (depending on the considered soundness
notion). Negligible soundness/knowledge error can always be achieved by
parallel repetition (see e.g. {[}Dam10{]}).

8

\textbf{3.2 The Fiat-Shamir transformation}

The \emph{Fiat-Shamir transformation} turns a Σ-protocol Σ into a
non-interactive proof system, denoted FS{[}Σ{]},

by replacing the verifier's random choice of \emph{c ∈C} with \emph{c}
:= \emph{H}(\emph{x, a}), where \emph{H} : \emph{X′→C} is a hash
function with a domain \emph{X′}that contains all pairs \emph{x′}=
(\emph{x, a}) with \emph{x ∈X} and \emph{a} produced by \emph{P}. In
other words, upon input \emph{x} and \emph{w}, the honest FS-prover
produces \emph{π} = (\emph{a, z}) by running the two-stage Σ-protocol
prover \emph{P} but using \emph{c} = \emph{H}(\emph{x, a}) as challenge
(i.e., as input to the second stage). In case Σ is not statistically
correct, the

above process of producing \emph{π} = (\emph{a, z}) is repeated
sufficiently many times until \emph{V} (\emph{x, a, H}(\emph{x,
a})\emph{, z}) is satisfied

(or some bound is reached). In either case, we will write this as

\emph{π} = (\emph{a, z}) \emph{←PH F S}(\emph{x, w}) \emph{.}

We may write as \emph{VH F S}(\emph{x, π}) the FS-verifier's check
whether \emph{V} (\emph{x, a, H}(\emph{x, a})\emph{, z}) is satisfied or
not. In the

security analysis, the hash function \emph{H} is modeled by a random
oracle, i.e. by oracle access to a uniformly

random \emph{H} : \emph{X′→C}.

When considering an \emph{adversary A} that tries to \emph{forge} a
proof for some instance \emph{x ∈X}, one can distinguish between an
\emph{arbitrary but fixed x}, and an \emph{x} that is \emph{chosen} by
\emph{A} and output along with \emph{a} in case of Σ-protocols,
respectively along with \emph{π} in case of the Fiat-Shamir
transformation. If \emph{x} is fixed then the adversary is called

\emph{static}, otherwise it is called \emph{adaptive}. For the typical
security definitions for Σ-protocols this distinction

between a static and an adaptive \emph{A} makes no difference (see
Lemmas 12 and 15 below), but for the Fiat-Shamir transformation it
(potentially) does.

\textbf{3.3 The generic security reduction}

Since an adaptive adversary is clearly not less powerful than a static
adversary, we restrict our attention

for the moment to the adaptive case. Recall that such an adaptive
FS-adversary \emph{A} outputs the instance \emph{x ∈X} along with the
proof \emph{π} = (\emph{a, z}), and the figure of merit is the
probability that \emph{x, a, z} satisfies \emph{V} (\emph{x, a,
H}(\emph{x, a})\emph{, z}). Thus, we can simply apply Theorem 2, with
(\emph{x, a}) playing the role of what is referred to

as \emph{x} in the theorem statement, to obtain the existence of an
adaptive \emph{Σ}-adversary \emph{SA}that produces (\emph{x, a}) in a
first stage, and upon receiving a random challenge \emph{c} produces
\emph{z}, such that for any \emph{x◦∈X}

1

\begin{quote}
Pr

\emph{c} \emph{x}=\emph{x◦∧V} (\emph{x, a, c, z}) : (\emph{x, a, z})
\emph{←⟨SA, c⟩} ≳\emph{O}(\emph{q}2) Pr \emph{x}=\emph{x◦∧V} (\emph{x,
a, H}(\emph{x, a})\emph{, z}) : (\emph{x, a, z}) \emph{←AH} \emph{,}
\end{quote}

where the approximate inequality hides a term that is bounded by
2\emph{q\textbar C\textbar{}}when summed over all \emph{x◦∈X}.

Understanding that \emph{x} is given to \emph{V} along with the first
message \emph{a} but also treating it as an output of \emph{SA}, while
\emph{V}'s output \emph{v} is its decision to accept or not, we write
this as

1

