9

security definitions, security of a Σ-protocol (against a dishonest
prover) implies security of its Fiat-Shamir

\begin{quote}
transform.

We summarize here the above basic transformation from an adaptive
FS-adversary \emph{A} to an adaptive\emph{Σ}-adversary \emph{SA}.

\textbf{Theorem 8} \emph{There exists a black-box quantum
polynomial-time two-stage quantum algorithm S such that for any adaptive
Fiat-Shamir adversary A, making q queries to a uniformly random function
H with appropriate domain and range, and for any x◦∈X:}
\end{quote}

1

\begin{quote}
Pr \emph{x}=\emph{x◦∧v} = \emph{accept} : (\emph{x, v}) \emph{←⟨SA, V⟩}
≳\emph{O}(\emph{q}2) Pr \emph{x}=\emph{x◦∧VH F S}(\emph{x, π}) :
(\emph{x, π}) \emph{←AH} \emph{,}
\end{quote}

\emph{where the} ≳\emph{hides a term that is bounded by}

\begin{quote}
2\emph{q\textbar C\textbar when summed over all x◦.}
\end{quote}

Below, we apply the above general reduction to the respective standard
definitions for \emph{soundness} and \emph{proof}

\emph{of knowledge}. Each property comes in the variants
\emph{computational} and \emph{statistical}, for guarantees against

computationally bounded or unbounded adversaries respectively, and one
may consider the static or the

\begin{quote}
adaptive case.

\textbf{3.4 Preservation of soundness}

Let Σ = (\emph{P, V}) be a Σ-protocol for a relation \emph{R}, and let
FS{[}Σ{]} be its Fiat-Shamir transformation. We set \emph{L} :=
\emph{\{x ∈X \textbar{} ∃w ∈W} : \emph{R}(\emph{x, w})\emph{\}}. It is
understood that \emph{P} and \emph{V}, as well as \emph{R} and thus
\emph{L}, may depend on a security parameter \emph{η}. We note that in
the following definition, we overload notation a bit by writing \emph{A}
for both for the ordinary static and for the adaptive adversary (even
though a given \emph{A} is usually either static or adaptive).
\end{quote}

\textbf{Definition 9} Σ \emph{is \textbf{(computationally/statistically)
sound} if there exists a negligible function µ}(\emph{η}) \emph{such}

\begin{quote}
\emph{that for any (quantum polynomial-time/unbounded) adversary A and
any η ∈}N\emph{:}
\end{quote}

Pr {[}\emph{⟨A, V}(\emph{x})\emph{⟩}= \emph{accept}{]}
\emph{≤µ}(\emph{η})

\begin{quote}
\emph{for all x /∈L; respectively, in case of an} \textbf{adaptive}
\emph{A:}
\end{quote}

Pr {[}\emph{x ̸∈L ∧v} = \emph{accept} : (\emph{x, v}) \emph{←⟨A, V⟩}{]}
\emph{≤µ}(\emph{η}) \emph{.}

FS{[}Σ{]} \emph{is \textbf{(computationally/statistically) sound} if
there exists a negligible function µ}(\emph{η}) \emph{and a constant}

\begin{quote}
\emph{e such that for any (quantum polynomial-time/unbounded) adversary
A and any η ∈}N\emph{:}
\end{quote}

Pr

\begin{quote}
\emph{H VH F S}(\emph{x, π}) : \emph{π ←AH≤qeµ}(\emph{η})

\emph{for all x /∈L; respectively, in case of an} \textbf{adaptive}
\emph{A:}
\end{quote}

Pr

\begin{quote}
\emph{H} \emph{VH F S}(\emph{x, π}) \emph{∧x /∈L} : (\emph{x, π})
\emph{←AH} \emph{≤qeµ}(\emph{η}) \emph{.}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Remark 10} Note that for the soundness of FS{[}Σ{]}, the
adversary \emph{A}'s success probability may unavoidably grow with the
number \emph{q} of oracle queries, but we require that it grows only
polynomially in \emph{q}.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Remark 11} In line with Section 2, the description of a quantum
algorithm \emph{A} is understood to include the initial state
\emph{\textbar φ}0\emph{⟩}. As such, when quantifying over all \emph{A}
it is understood that this includes a quantification over all
\emph{\textbar φ}0\emph{⟩}as well. This stays true when considering
\emph{A} to be quantum polynomial-time, which means that the unitaries
\emph{Ai} can be computed by polynomial-time quantum circuits, and
\emph{q} is polynomial in size, but
\end{quote}

does not put any restriction on \emph{\textbar φ}0\emph{⟩}.14This is in
line with {[}Unr12, Def. 1{]}, which explicitly spells out this
quantification.

14In other words, \emph{A} is then \emph{non-uniform} quantum
polynomial-time with \emph{quantum} advice.

10

We consider the following to be folklore knowledge; for completeness, we
still give a proof in Appendix A. \textbf{Lemma 12} \emph{If} Σ \emph{is
computationally/statistically sound for} static \emph{adversaries then
it is also computation-ally/statistically sound for} adaptive
\emph{adversaries.}

The following is now an immediate application of Theorem 8 and the above
observation regarding static and adaptive security for Σ-protocols.

