\begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright
We now recall the definition of a proof of knowledge, sometimes also
referred to as (witness) extractability,

tailored to the case of a negligible ``knowledge error''. Informally,
the requirement is that if \emph{A} succeeds in proving an instance
\emph{x}, then by using \emph{A} as a black-box only it is possible to
extract a witness for \emph{x}. In case of an arbitrary but fixed
\emph{x}, this property is formalized in a rather straightforward way;
however, in case of

an adaptive \emph{A}, the formalization is somewhat subtle, because one
can then not refer to \emph{the x} for which \emph{A} manages to produce
a proof. We adopt the approach (though not the precise formalization)
from {[}Unr17{]},

which requires \emph{x} to satisfy an arbitrary but fixed predicate.

\textbf{Definition 14} Σ \emph{is a \textbf{(computational/statistical)
proof of knowledge} if there exists a quantum}

\emph{polynomial-time black-box `knowledge extractor' K, a polynomial
p}(\emph{η})\emph{, a constant d ≥}0\emph{, and a negligible function
κ}(\emph{η}) \emph{such that for any (quantum polynomial-time/unbounded)
adversary A, any η ∈}N \emph{and any x ∈X:}

1

\begin{quote}
Pr (\emph{x, w}) \emph{∈R} : \emph{w
←KA}(\emph{x})\emph{≥p}(\emph{η})\emph{·} Pr {[}\emph{⟨A,
V}(\emph{x})\emph{⟩}= \emph{accept}{]}\emph{d −κ}(\emph{η}) ;
\end{quote}
\end{minipage}} \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\emph{respectively, in case of an} \textbf{adaptive} \emph{A:}

